<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamsetji Tata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata was an Indian industrialist and philanthropist who founded the Tata Group, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s largest conglomerate. Born in Navsari into a poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a> family of Zoroastrian priests, he broke with the priestly tradition, joined his father's trading firm and founded his own trading company in 1868. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a> Hotel in Mumbai in 1903, India's first hotel with electricity. His plans for an iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> company, a hydroelectric plant and a research institute were realised after his death as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tata-steel">Tata Steel</a>, Tata Power and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-institute-of-science">Indian Institute of Science</a>, and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamshedpur">Jamshedpur</a> bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamsetji-tata.jpg" alt="Jamsetji Tata" width="330" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Publisher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_J._N._Tata_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata was born on 3 March 1839 in Navsari, to a respectable but poor family of Zoroastrian priests. His father, Nusserwanji Ratanji Tata, had broken with the priestly tradition to become the first businessman in the family. Jamsetji's mother tongue was Gujarati. His parents saw that he was gifted in mental arithmetic, and he joined his father in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> at the age of 14 and enrolled at Elphinstone College, completing his studies as a Green Scholar. He married Hirabai Daboo while still a student. After graduating in 1858 he joined his father's export-trading firm and helped set up branches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His father sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to learn how business was done there, including the opium trade. Tata saw instead that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> industry was booming. He worked in his father's company until he was 29 and then founded a trading company in 1868 with ₹21,000 of capital. In 1869 he bought a bankrupt oil mill at Chinchpokli, converted it to a cotton mill called Alexandra Mill, and sold it two years later at a profit. In 1874 he floated the Central India Spinning, Weaving, and Manufacturing Company in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>, and people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, the Cottonopolis of India, scorned the unconventional choice of place.</p><p>In 1885 a company he floated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> to distribute Indian textiles to nearby French colonies free of duty failed for lack of demand. He then bought the Dharamsi Mills at Kurla, resold them, and bought the Advance Mills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>, named for its up-to-date machinery. He supported the principles of Swadeshism before the Swadeshi movement began in 1905, and named a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> mill the Swadeshi Mill, meant to produce cloth to match Manchester's. He tried to improve the cultivation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and was the first to introduce the ring spindle into his mills, which replaced the throstle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tata set himself four aims: an iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> company, a world-class learning institution, a unique hotel and a hydroelectric plant. Only the hotel was completed in his lifetime, when the Taj Mahal Hotel opened on the Colaba waterfront in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> on 3 December 1903. His successors achieved the other three. He gave generously to education and healthcare. He fell seriously ill on a business trip to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1900 and died at Bad Nauheim on 19 May 1904, and he was buried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a> burial ground in Brookwood Cemetery, Woking, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. His sons Dorab and Ratan continued his work, and Dorab succeeded him as chair of the Tata Group. Jawaharlal Nehru called him a One-Man Planning Commission.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jamsetji Tata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Families such as the Tatas, whose founder <b>Jamsetji Tata</b> built the Taj Mahal Palace hotel in Bombay and planned India’s first great steelworks, made the Parsis one of the most prominent communities in India’s modern economy.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Indians also built industry: in 1877 <b>Jamsetji Tata</b> began a spinning and weaving company in Bombay whose finer yarn, spun from Egyptian cotton on American ring spindles, could compete with British imports, and in 1908 his son’s Tata Iron and Steel Company began a steelworks at Jamshedpur, its…</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Navsari, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Bad Nauheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.95,72.92&amp;year=1881" aria-label="Navsari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6511 1389 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jamsetji Tata"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6961 1659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6961 1659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.95,72.92&amp;year=1881">What was here in 1881? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
